If the sine rule gives $\sin\theta>1$ — the swung side $a$ is shorter than $b\sin A$ — no triangle exists.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc,arrows.meta}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (6.8,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.77,2.64);
\coordinate (F) at (3.77,0);
\coordinate (E) at ($(B)+(305:2)$);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (A)--(X);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$b$};
\draw (B)--(E) node[midway,left] {$a$};
\draw[dashed] ($(B)+(230:2)$) arc (230:310:2);
\draw[dashed] (B)--(F) node[midway,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$b\sin A$};
\coordinate (r1) at ($(F)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\coordinate (r2) at ($(F)!0.3cm!(X)$);
\draw (r1)--($(r1)+(r2)-(F)$)--(r2);
\pic[draw,"$A$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle=X--A--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two triangles occur when $b\sin A<a<b$, because the swung side $a$ then reaches the base in two places.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc,arrows.meta}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (X) at (6.8,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.77,2.64);
\coordinate (C) at (1.96,0);
\coordinate (D) at (5.58,0);
\coordinate (F) at (3.77,0);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (A)--(X);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,above left] {$b$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,above left] {$a$};
\draw (B)--(D) node[midway,above right] {$a$};
\draw[dashed] ($(B)+(228:3.2)$) arc (228:312:3.2);
\draw[dashed] (B)--(F) node[midway,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$b\sin A$};
\coordinate (r1) at ($(F)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\coordinate (r2) at ($(F)!0.3cm!(X)$);
\draw (r1)--($(r1)+(r2)-(F)$)--(r2);
\fill (C) circle (2pt);
\fill (D) circle (2pt);
\pic[draw,"$A$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle=X--A--B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

